// SECURITY HEADER CHECKLIST
// ============================================================

/*
 *
 * IMPLEMENTED:
 *
 * [x] X-Content-Type-Options
 *
 * [x] X-Frame-Options
 *
 * [x] Content-Security-Policy
 *
 * [x] Strict-Transport-Security
 *
 * [x] Referrer-Policy
 *
 * [x] Permissions-Policy
 *
 * [x] Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy
 *
 * [x] Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy
 *
 * [x] X-XSS-Protection for legacy clients
 *
 * [x] Remove X-Powered-By
 *
 * [x] Configurable policies
 *
 * [x] Unit tests
 *
 *
 * SECURITY PURPOSE:
 *
 *
 * X-Content-Type-Options
 *     ↓
 * MIME sniffing protection
 *
 *
 * X-Frame-Options
 *     ↓
 * Clickjacking protection
 *
 *
 * Content-Security-Policy
 *     ↓
 * Restricts executable/resource origins
 *
 *
 * Strict-Transport-Security
 *     ↓
 * Forces HTTPS after browser policy is cached
 *
 *
 * Referrer-Policy
 *     ↓
 * Limits URL/referrer leakage
 *
 *
 * Permissions-Policy
 *     ↓
 * Restricts browser capabilities
 *
 *
 * Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy
 *     ↓
 * Restricts cross-origin resource loading
 *
 *
 * Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy
 *     ↓
 * Provides browsing-context isolation
 *
 */

// ============================================================
// IMPORTANT PRODUCTION NOTES
// ============================================================

/*
 *
 * 1. HSTS
 *
 * Do NOT enable HSTS blindly on an environment that is not
 * fully HTTPS-enabled.
 *
 *
 *
 * 2. CSP
 *
 * Do not add:
 *
 *     script-src 'unsafe-inline'
 *
 * or:
 *
 *     script-src 'unsafe-eval'
 *
 * in production unless there is a documented requirement.
 *
 *
 *
 * 3. REST API
 *
 * For a pure REST API, the strongest CSP is often:
 *
 *     default-src 'none';
 *     frame-ancestors 'none'
 *
 *
 *
 * 4. Swagger
 *
 * If Swagger UI is served by this NestJS application, the CSP
 * may need additional directives for Swagger's scripts,
 * styles, fonts, and API connections.
 *
 *
 *
 * 5. Proxy
 *
 * If HTTPS is terminated by Nginx, a load balancer, Cloudflare,
 * or another reverse proxy, configure the application to
 * correctly understand the forwarded protocol before relying
 * on HTTPS-only behavior.
 *
 *
 *
 * 6. Cookies
 *
 * Security headers do NOT replace secure cookie configuration.
 *
 * Authentication cookies should additionally consider:
 *
 *     Secure
 *     HttpOnly
 *     SameSite
 *
 *
 *
 * 7. CORS
 *
 * Security headers also do NOT replace proper CORS policy.
 *
 * Avoid:
 *
 *     Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
 *
 * for authenticated/private APIs.
 *
 */

